---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { PUBLISHER, SITE } from '../consts';
import { simplePageBreadcrumb } from '../lib/schema';
const description = 'How App-Tipps.com reports, reviews and scores, how we handle corrections and conflicts of interest, and how advertising is kept separate from editorial.';
const url = `${SITE.url}/editorial-policy/`;
---
<Base title="Editorial Policy"
      {description} canonical={url} schema={[
        {
          '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': `${url}#webpage`, url,
          name: 'Editorial Policy', description,
          isPartOf: { '@id': `${SITE.url}/#website` },
        },
        simplePageBreadcrumb('/editorial-policy/', 'Editorial Policy'),
      ]}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> / Editorial Policy</nav>
    <div class="prose" style="padding:34px 0 10px">
      <h1>Editorial Policy</h1>
      <p class="updated">Last updated 14 August 2026.</p>

      <p>
        App-Tipps.com covers mainstream mobile games and applications. This page sets out how we report,
        how we review, how we correct mistakes, and how our commercial arrangements work. Questions go to
        {' '}<a href={`mailto:${PUBLISHER.editorialEmail}`}>{PUBLISHER.editorialEmail}</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>What we publish</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Game and app reviews</li>
        <li>Game guides and app tips</li>
        <li>Mobile industry news</li>
        <li>Comparisons between competing apps and games</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Every article is reviewed and approved by an editor before publication. Research and drafting
        tools may assist the process, but a human editor is responsible for checking factual claims,
        deciding what is published and approving the finished article. We do not republish or lightly
        rework other publications' articles as our own.
      </p>

      <h2>Reporting standards</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>We seek verifiable sources and credit the original reporter when a story is not ours.</li>
        <li>Rumours and leaks are labelled as rumours and leaks.</li>
        <li>We do not write headlines that the article does not support.</li>
        <li>We answer questions about our accuracy rather than ignoring them.</li>
        <li>We honour reasonable requests for source anonymity.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How we review and score</h2>
      <p>
        First-person claims about using an app or game are only made when that use actually took place.
        Review scores are editorial assessments based on the article's evaluation, available product
        information and, where applicable, hands-on use. Articles based primarily on research are not
        presented as first-person testing. The editorial team as a whole is responsible for what appears
        on the site. Games are assessed on gameplay, monetisation, graphics and audio, among other factors.
        Scores run to a maximum of 5.0:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>4.3 and above</strong> &mdash; very good, worth your time</li>
        <li><strong>3.5 to 4.29</strong> &mdash; good</li>
        <li><strong>2.0 to 3.49</strong> &mdash; average</li>
        <li><strong>below 2.0</strong> &mdash; poor</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Mobile games and apps change constantly. When a significant update lands, we may revisit a title
        and change its score. The publication and update dates on every article tell you how current it is.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        We correct errors rather than quietly deleting them. Send the article URL and the problem to
        {' '}<a href={`mailto:${PUBLISHER.editorialEmail}`}>{PUBLISHER.editorialEmail}</a>. Substantive
        corrections are noted on the article, and the update date changes so you can see it happened.
      </p>

      <h2>Bylines and pen names</h2>
      <p>
        App-Tipps uses consistent editorial bylines and pen names so readers can follow work published
        under the same name over time. The <strong>App-Tipps Editorial</strong> byline is used for work
        produced or reviewed collectively by the editorial team rather than attributed to an individual
        byline. Editorial responsibility for everything on this site rests with {PUBLISHER.legalName}.
        You can browse our bylines on the <a href="/authors/">Authors page</a> or read more on our
        <a href="/about/">About page</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Advertising, affiliates and independence</h2>
      <p>
        We keep a strict separation between advertising and editorial. Advertising is labelled as
        advertising. Articles containing affiliate links carry a disclosure at the top.
      </p>
      <p>
        We earn money three ways: display advertising, affiliate commission, and occasional sponsored
        content that is clearly marked as sponsored. None of these buy a verdict. If a product we earn
        commission on is bad, we say so. If a product we have no relationship with is excellent, we say
        that too.
      </p>
      <p>
        We do not accept payment to remove or amend a negative review, and we do not give advertisers
        advance sight of editorial coverage.
      </p>

      <h2>What we will not publish</h2>
      <p>
        We do not cover gambling, real-money wagering, or schemes presented as easy ways to make money.
        We do not link to modified APKs, cheat tools, or anything that breaks a platform's terms of
        service. We do not publish content aimed at children under 13.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
